{"result": {"response": {"id": "resp_06ed8ffca461a40c0169bb426ba708819182a330341610e94d", "created": 1773879915, "model": "gpt-5.2-2025-12-11", "object": "chat.completion", "system_fingerprint": null, "choices": [{"finish_reason": "stop", "index": 0, "message": {"content": "{\"title\":\"Diagnostic vs. Screening Mammography Community (Symptoms, Techniques, and Clinical Roles)\",\"summary\":\"This community centers on mammography as a clinical imaging workflow, with the **Diagnostic Mammogram** as the hub connecting patient-presented breast symptoms, follow-up from screening findings, specialized imaging techniques, and clinician/technologist roles. Screening mammograms are positioned as routine, asymptomatic early-detection tools within breast cancer screening, while diagnostic mammograms are described as more targeted, often reviewed immediately by a radiologist, and used to evaluate symptoms (e.g., lump, pain, nipple changes, breast shape/size change) or an abnormal area seen on screening. Supporting entities describe the technical basis (low-dose x-rays, compression plates, 2D/3D modalities), operational roles (radiologists and technologists), and population context (screening emphasis on people assigned female at birth; people assigned male at birth generally not routinely screened). [Data: Entities (3622, 3621, 3620, 3779, 3664,+more); Relationships (7420, 7719, 7417, 7419, 7487,+more)]\",\"findings\":[{\"summary\":\"Diagnostic mammography is the central, higher-resolution follow-up pathway for symptoms or suspicious findings\",\"explanation\":\"Diagnostic mammography is described as distinct from screening mammography and typically used when there are breast symptoms or when a screening mammogram identifies an abnormal area needing closer evaluation. It is positioned as a targeted evaluation that can involve additional focused views and immediate interpretive decision-making. [Data: Entities (3622, 3744, 3621, 3645, 3770); Relationships (7719, 7424, 7421, 7420, 7419)]\\n\\nA key operational difference noted is that a radiologist may review diagnostic mammogram images while the patient waits to decide whether further images or additional testing are needed immediately, underscoring diagnostic mammography’s role as an active problem-solving exam rather than a purely batch-screening workflow. [Data: Entities (3622); Relationships (7420)]\\n\\nThe diagnostic exam is also explicitly framed as supporting clinical assessment—helping evaluate whether a tumor may be present and estimate the size/characteristics of an abnormality—indicating its role in guiding subsequent evaluation and treatment planning when concerns exist. [Data: Entities (3622)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Symptom-driven indications form a tight cluster around diagnostic mammography\",\"explanation\":\"Multiple symptom entities directly link to diagnostic mammography as reasons the exam is performed, including breast lump and breast pain, nipple discharge, nipple thickening, and changes in breast shape or size. This indicates that the diagnostic pathway is triggered by both palpable symptoms and visible/structural changes. [Data: Entities (3639, 3640, 3642, 3641, 3643,+more); Relationships (7421, 7422, 7424, 7423, 7425,+more)]\\n\\nThe data also groups these as “symptoms of breast cancer,” reinforcing that the same symptom set is clinically treated as sufficiently concerning to prompt diagnostic imaging rather than routine screening alone. [Data: Entities (3779); Relationships (7806)]\\n\\nIn practical terms, this community representation emphasizes that diagnostic mammography is not only about imaging technique but also about triage logic: symptoms lead directly to more detailed imaging rather than waiting for routine screening intervals. [Data: Entities (3622, 3779); Relationships (7421, 7422, 7423, 7424, 7425,+more)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Screening mammography functions as the upstream filter; diagnostic mammography investigates ‘abnormal areas’\",\"explanation\":\"Screening mammograms are characterized as routine exams performed when there are no signs or symptoms, serving broader breast cancer screening goals for early detection. Results are also described as not usually immediate (taking a few days), distinguishing screening’s workflow and intent from diagnostic evaluation. [Data: Entities (3621, 3628); Relationships (7417)]\\n\\nA specific handoff is documented: an “abnormal area” identified on a screening mammogram may then be examined more closely, prompting diagnostic mammography. This creates a clear pipeline from population screening to individualized diagnostic workup based on imaging findings. [Data: Entities (3645, 3770, 3621); Relationships (7419, 7786)]\\n\\nDiagnostic mammograms are further described as targeting specific areas that may not be clearly seen on screening mammograms, implying a refinement step where the diagnostic protocol increases focus and detail to resolve uncertainty from the initial screen. [Data: Entities (3744, 3622); Relationships (7719)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Diagnostic mammography relies on specialized positioning and image-acquisition techniques to clarify findings\",\"explanation\":\"The community includes several named techniques associated with diagnostic mammography—extra compression, magnification views, and rolling the breast—each intended to better visualize areas of concern or extend imaging coverage. This suggests a toolkit of acquisition strategies to resolve ambiguity or highlight subtle findings. [Data: Entities (3745, 3746, 3747, 3622); Relationships (7721, 7722, 7723)]\\n\\nBilateral imaging is explicitly represented as part of diagnostic mammography when needed, supporting the idea that diagnostic protocols can expand from a focal area to both breasts depending on clinical context. [Data: Entities (3737, 3622); Relationships (7717)]\\n\\nCompression hardware is also tied to diagnostic mammography: mammography involves pressing the breast between two plates, and diagnostic exams may add “extra compression” beyond standard compression to enhance visualization. [Data: Entities (3750, 3626, 3745); Relationships (7771, 7721)]\"},{\"summary\":\"The technical foundation: low-dose x-rays, compression plates, and 2D/3D mammography options\",\"explanation\":\"Mammography is defined as an x-ray–based imaging test that creates detailed pictures of breast tissue, explicitly using low-dose x-rays. The exam process includes positioning the breast and compressing it between two plates while multiple images are taken. [Data: Entities (3620, 3623, 3626); Relationships (7410, 7411)]\\n\\nThe data also enumerates modality formats, including standard 2D digital mammography and 3D mammography (tomosynthesis), indicating that mammography in this community is not a single fixed modality but a family of related imaging approaches. [Data: Entities (3624, 3625, 3774); Relationships (7415, 7416, 7804)]\\n\\nThis technical layer supports both screening and diagnostic workflows, with diagnostic mammography distinguished not by abandoning core mammography physics, but by applying additional targeted views and techniques to answer a focused clinical question. [Data: Entities (3620, 3622); Relationships (7719)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Clinical roles separate image acquisition from interpretation, with technologists and radiologists as key operators\",\"explanation\":\"A radiologist is described as evaluating the diagnostic mammogram while the patient waits to determine whether more testing is needed immediately, emphasizing rapid interpretive decision-making as part of diagnostic care. [Data: Relationships (7420)]\\n\\nA breast diagnostic radiologist is explicitly identified as interpreting mammograms (and other imaging tests) and performing needle biopsies as needed, positioning this role as a specialized interpretive and procedural extension of imaging evaluation. [Data: Entities (3657); Relationships (7485)]\\n\\nTechnologists are documented as operators of imaging equipment, including mammogram units and ultrasound machines, indicating an operational division of labor where technologists acquire images and clinicians interpret and decide on next steps. [Data: Entities (3660, 3668, 3669); Relationships (7506, 7508)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Population context: screening emphasis and exceptions are explicitly stated for people assigned female vs. male at birth\",\"explanation\":\"The community states that screening recommendations (as represented in the text) focus on people assigned female at birth, aligning screening mammography with higher-frequency occurrence and standardized screening practices in that population. [Data: Entities (3663); Relationships (7473)]\\n\\nIt also notes that people assigned male at birth can develop breast cancer but generally do not undergo regular mammogram screening, indicating a population-specific screening norm and a different threshold for imaging driven more by symptoms and clinical concern than by routine screening schedules. [Data: Entities (3664, 3620); Relationships (7487)]\\n\\nTogether, these relationships frame mammography not only as a technical procedure but as a policy- and practice-shaped pathway where who gets screened routinely versus evaluated diagnostically depends on population context and clinical presentation. [Data: Entities (3621, 3663, 3664); Relationships (7417, 7473, 7487)]\"}],\"rating\":3.8,\"rating_explanation\":\"The community’s impact is moderate because it represents a clinically significant diagnostic and screening workflow that influences healthcare decision-making, but it does not indicate illicit activity, emerging threats, or contentious reputation concerns in the provided data.\"}", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null, "reasoning_content": "**Structuring technology modalities**\n\nI'm looking at five technology modalities: low-dose x-rays, 2D digital, 3D tomosynthesis, and mammogram units. \n\nFor roles, there are six professionals involved, includi